1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,857
October first, nineteen forty-eight.

2
00:00:02,857 --> 00:00:10,708
Second Lieutenant George Gorman, twenty-five, a wartime fighter pilot, flies a Mustang over Fargo, North Dakota.

3
00:00:10,708 --> 00:00:15,966
For the next twenty-seven minutes, he says, a light fights him in the dark.

4
00:00:16,433 --> 00:00:18,945
The Air Force would call it a balloon.

5
00:00:18,945 --> 00:00:20,268
Gorman never agreed.

6
00:00:20,268 --> 00:00:28,533
He called it a dogfight, and in his statement he wrote that its moves showed, quote, a definite thought behind its maneuvers.

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:31,204
So tonight, we fly the circuit.

8
00:00:31,204 --> 00:00:34,974
Every airfield has one, a pattern of laps in the sky.

9
00:00:34,974 --> 00:00:40,166
We take the dogfight one leg at a time, from downwind to the final stall.

10
00:00:40,633 --> 00:00:44,535
At each leg, we ask what is evidence, and what is rumor.

11
00:00:44,535 --> 00:00:51,433
Then we put the suspects on trial: a lighted balloon, the planet Jupiter, and a craft nobody built.

12
00:00:51,899 --> 00:00:53,339
At the end, a verdict.

13
00:00:53,339 --> 00:00:57,133
Let's join the pattern, at nine o'clock on a Friday night.

14
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:03,243
Friday, October first.

15
00:01:03,243 --> 00:01:04,887
Hector Airport, Fargo.

16
00:01:04,887 --> 00:01:13,854
The North Dakota Air National Guard's squadron has flown in from a cross-country trip, and the other pilots have landed.

17
00:01:13,854 --> 00:01:17,366
Gorman stays up, to log some night flying time.

18
00:01:17,833 --> 00:01:18,970
The sky is clear.

19
00:01:18,970 --> 00:01:25,723
He circles the lighted football stadium, a bright landmark in the dark, before turning for the field.

20
00:01:25,723 --> 00:01:29,066
Around nine o'clock, the tower clears him to land.

21
00:01:29,533 --> 00:01:32,103
He checks the traffic with the tower.

22
00:01:32,103 --> 00:01:36,409
The controllers work for the Civil Aeronautics Administration.

23
00:01:36,409 --> 00:01:39,952
They say the only other aircraft up is a Piper Cub.

24
00:01:39,952 --> 00:01:43,633
Gorman can see it, about five hundred feet below him.

25
00:01:44,099 --> 00:01:45,258
Remember that Cub.

26
00:01:45,258 --> 00:01:49,700
Its pilot and his passenger are about to become two of the witnesses.

27
00:01:50,166 --> 00:01:52,706
Then, on his right, a light passes.

28
00:01:52,706 --> 00:01:56,408
Gorman takes it for the taillight of another plane.

29
00:01:56,408 --> 00:01:57,714
He asks the tower.

30
00:01:57,714 --> 00:02:00,400
The tower knows of no other aircraft.

31
00:02:00,866 --> 00:02:02,234
He turns toward it.

32
00:02:02,234 --> 00:02:04,826
As he closes in, he sees what it is.

33
00:02:04,826 --> 00:02:09,793
A light, clear white, perfectly round, with sharp edges, and no fuzz.

34
00:02:09,793 --> 00:02:14,833
By his estimate, six to eight inches across at about a thousand yards.

35
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:17,122
It is blinking on and off.

36
00:02:17,122 --> 00:02:20,207
Then, as he gets closer, the blinking stops.

37
00:02:20,207 --> 00:02:24,133
The light goes steady, and it banks sharply to the left.

38
00:02:24,599 --> 00:02:26,479
Gorman is a fighter pilot.

39
00:02:26,479 --> 00:02:28,937
He does what a fighter pilot does.

40
00:02:28,937 --> 00:02:30,599
He decides to catch it.

41
00:02:31,066 --> 00:02:37,951
He pushes the throttle forward and dives, sixty inches of manifold pressure, all the Mustang has.

42
00:02:37,951 --> 00:02:40,010
He cannot close the distance.

43
00:02:40,010 --> 00:02:43,133
The light climbs away, and banks left again.

44
00:02:43,599 --> 00:02:48,808
He tries to cut it off, to turn inside its path and meet it on the far side.

45
00:02:48,808 --> 00:02:49,493
He cannot.

46
00:02:49,493 --> 00:02:52,166
Each time, the light is somewhere else.

47
00:02:52,633 --> 00:02:56,028
In the tower, controllers Lloyd Jensen and H.

48
00:02:56,028 --> 00:02:56,179
E.

49
00:02:56,179 --> 00:02:57,386
Johnson look up.

50
00:02:57,386 --> 00:03:01,083
They see a fast light, and the Mustang beside it.

51
00:03:01,083 --> 00:03:05,233
Quote, a round light, perfectly formed, no fuzzy edges.

52
00:03:05,699 --> 00:03:07,425
And they see the gap grow.

53
00:03:07,425 --> 00:03:10,676
The light increases its spacing from the fighter.

54
00:03:10,676 --> 00:03:11,937
It is not drifting.

55
00:03:11,937 --> 00:03:12,866
It is leaving.

56
00:03:13,333 --> 00:03:14,786
The Cub's pilot, Dr.

57
00:03:14,786 --> 00:03:14,931
A.

58
00:03:14,931 --> 00:03:15,077
E.

59
00:03:15,077 --> 00:03:19,000
Cannon, and his passenger, Einar Nielson, also see it.

60
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:24,594
The swift light passes above them, much faster, they say, than the fifty-one.

61
00:03:24,594 --> 00:03:28,300
They land and watch from the tower with binoculars.

62
00:03:28,766 --> 00:03:32,587
So beyond Gorman, there are four civilian witnesses.

63
00:03:32,587 --> 00:03:37,143
Two more Civil Aeronautics employees report seeing it as well.

64
00:03:37,143 --> 00:03:41,699
Six witnesses besides the pilot, in the air and on the ground.

65
00:03:42,166 --> 00:03:42,872
No sound.

66
00:03:42,872 --> 00:03:43,577
No smell.

67
00:03:43,577 --> 00:03:44,439
No exhaust.

68
00:03:44,439 --> 00:03:48,123
And no change on any instrument in the cockpit.

69
00:03:48,123 --> 00:03:50,866
Gorman reports all of it afterward.

70
00:03:51,333 --> 00:03:55,466
He puts the light's speed at up to four hundred miles an hour.

71
00:03:55,466 --> 00:03:59,266
That is fast, but not impossible in nineteen forty-eight.

72
00:03:59,266 --> 00:04:01,599
It is the turning that bothers him.

73
00:04:01,599 --> 00:04:02,800
Hold that thought.

74
00:04:03,266 --> 00:04:04,924
Now the pattern tightens.

75
00:04:04,924 --> 00:04:09,566
Next leg: final approach, and the moment the light stops running away.

76
00:04:10,033 --> 00:04:13,186
At about seven thousand feet, the chase changes.

77
00:04:13,186 --> 00:04:18,441
The light that has been running from him turns right, and comes straight at him.

78
00:04:18,441 --> 00:04:18,966
Head-on.

79
00:04:19,433 --> 00:04:21,774
Gorman does what any pilot would.

80
00:04:21,774 --> 00:04:23,264
He dives to avoid it.

81
00:04:23,264 --> 00:04:29,366
The light passes over his canopy, about five hundred feet above him, by his judgement.

82
00:04:29,833 --> 00:04:30,969
He circles left.

83
00:04:30,969 --> 00:04:34,163
The light is now about a thousand feet above.

84
00:04:34,163 --> 00:04:35,866
He lines up on it again.

85
00:04:36,333 --> 00:04:40,434
In one of these hard turns, Gorman blacks out for a moment.

86
00:04:40,434 --> 00:04:43,215
Remember that when we weigh his account.

87
00:04:43,215 --> 00:04:47,733
A pilot who loses consciousness in a turn is not at his sharpest.

88
00:04:48,199 --> 00:04:49,682
A second head-on pass.

89
00:04:49,682 --> 00:04:52,984
This time the light does not slide away sideways.

90
00:04:52,984 --> 00:04:54,466
It shoots straight up.

91
00:04:54,933 --> 00:04:57,287
Gorman follows it into the climb.

92
00:04:57,287 --> 00:05:00,997
At about fourteen thousand feet, the Mustang stalls.

93
00:05:00,997 --> 00:05:02,566
The light keeps going.

94
00:05:03,033 --> 00:05:04,726
Nine twenty-seven p.m.

95
00:05:04,726 --> 00:05:06,649
Gorman loses sight of it.

96
00:05:06,649 --> 00:05:11,266
By the clock, the encounter has lasted twenty-seven minutes.

97
00:05:11,733 --> 00:05:13,393
He lands, and reports.

98
00:05:13,393 --> 00:05:15,656
His statement goes to Major D.

99
00:05:15,656 --> 00:05:15,807
C.

100
00:05:15,807 --> 00:05:16,260
Jones.

101
00:05:16,260 --> 00:05:20,033
The whole thing is on paper before he goes to bed.

102
00:05:20,500 --> 00:05:26,039
In it, he says the moves showed, quote, a definite thought behind its maneuvers.

103
00:05:26,039 --> 00:05:30,955
But he also says the light was, quote, governed by the laws of inertia.

104
00:05:30,955 --> 00:05:33,033
Keep that second line in mind.

105
00:05:33,500 --> 00:05:39,027
Within days, the Air Force's first UFO study, Project Sign, takes the case.

106
00:05:39,027 --> 00:05:43,669
Gorman's plane is checked, and a Geiger counter is run over it.

107
00:05:43,669 --> 00:05:45,733
It reads higher than normal.

108
00:05:46,199 --> 00:05:47,140
Radioactive?

109
00:05:47,140 --> 00:05:47,375
No.

110
00:05:47,375 --> 00:05:54,116
The reading is later explained as cosmic rays, picked up during time at high altitude.

111
00:05:54,116 --> 00:05:56,233
Pilots do pick up a little.

112
00:05:56,699 --> 00:05:58,974
The investigators judge the man.

113
00:05:58,974 --> 00:06:03,026
Quote, he did not make the impression of being a dreamer.

114
00:06:03,026 --> 00:06:07,433
A sincere and serious individual who was considerably puzzled.

115
00:06:07,899 --> 00:06:09,324
He is no fantasist.

116
00:06:09,324 --> 00:06:11,125
A wartime fighter pilot.

117
00:06:11,125 --> 00:06:13,975
A construction company manager by day.

118
00:06:13,975 --> 00:06:15,850
A Guard officer at night.

119
00:06:15,850 --> 00:06:22,899
He will retire as a lieutenant colonel in nineteen sixty-nine, and die in nineteen eighty-two.

120
00:06:23,366 --> 00:06:31,661
Edward Ruppelt, who later ran the Air Force's UFO project, calls it one of the three classics of nineteen forty-eight.

121
00:06:31,661 --> 00:06:36,933
The others are the Mantell crash, and the Chiles-Whitted airliner sighting.

122
00:06:37,399 --> 00:06:39,014
Look at what is missing.

123
00:06:39,014 --> 00:06:39,956
No photograph.

124
00:06:39,956 --> 00:06:40,764
No wreckage.

125
00:06:40,764 --> 00:06:42,917
No physical trace on the ground.

126
00:06:42,917 --> 00:06:44,666
What we have is testimony.

127
00:06:45,133 --> 00:06:47,071
But it is a lot of testimony.

128
00:06:47,071 --> 00:06:53,087
A pilot in the air, controllers in the tower, a doctor and his passenger in a small plane.

129
00:06:53,087 --> 00:06:56,229
All of them saw a fast, round light that night.

130
00:06:56,229 --> 00:06:57,566
Something was there.

131
00:06:58,033 --> 00:06:59,070
So what was it?

132
00:06:59,070 --> 00:07:01,420
The Air Force had an answer ready.

133
00:07:01,420 --> 00:07:03,633
And it was only ten minutes old.

134
00:07:04,100 --> 00:07:07,672
The official explanation is a lighted weather balloon.

135
00:07:07,672 --> 00:07:13,096
The Air Weather Service released one from Fargo at about eight-fifty that evening.

136
00:07:13,096 --> 00:07:17,000
That is roughly ten minutes before Gorman's first sighting.

137
00:07:17,466 --> 00:07:20,781
A small balloon with a lamp drifts with the wind.

138
00:07:20,781 --> 00:07:26,600
At night, with no horizon and no sense of size, it looks like a steady point of light.

139
00:07:27,066 --> 00:07:29,818
The early reviews add another suspect.

140
00:07:29,818 --> 00:07:32,786
Jupiter was bright in the sky that night.

141
00:07:32,786 --> 00:07:38,000
Project Sign and Project Grudge point to Jupiter and a balloon together.

142
00:07:38,466 --> 00:07:44,599
Astronomer Donald Menzel later argues for a balloon, plus a mirage of Jupiter.

143
00:07:44,599 --> 00:07:48,766
The final Blue Book label is simply: lighted balloon.

144
00:07:49,233 --> 00:07:53,343
Ruppelt gives a reason why the passes look so strange.

145
00:07:53,343 --> 00:08:01,639
When a fast plane overtakes a slow balloon, he explains, it can look like the object is rushing past head-on.

146
00:08:01,639 --> 00:08:03,466
The turns are all yours.

147
00:08:03,933 --> 00:08:05,227
Decades later, Dr.

148
00:08:05,227 --> 00:08:10,115
James McDonald reads the Blue Book file itself, in nineteen seventy.

149
00:08:10,115 --> 00:08:14,500
He finds explicit statements in it that discount the balloon.

150
00:08:14,966 --> 00:08:17,381
And Gorman himself never accepted it.

151
00:08:17,381 --> 00:08:23,647
He told friends he was never convinced he had spent twenty-seven minutes dueling with a balloon.

152
00:08:23,647 --> 00:08:25,866
So let's put the balloon on trial.

153
00:08:26,333 --> 00:08:28,895
Suspect one: the lighted weather balloon.

154
00:08:28,895 --> 00:08:30,145
The official answer.

155
00:08:30,145 --> 00:08:32,333
The one that sits on the paperwork.

156
00:08:32,799 --> 00:08:34,411
Here is what favors it.

157
00:08:34,411 --> 00:08:37,844
A lighted balloon really was released from Fargo.

158
00:08:37,844 --> 00:08:38,965
The timing fits.

159
00:08:38,965 --> 00:08:43,799
And it is a small, round point of light, much like what he describes.

160
00:08:44,266 --> 00:08:47,390
Night flying is also a trap for the eyes.

161
00:08:47,390 --> 00:08:48,228
No horizon.

162
00:08:48,228 --> 00:08:49,219
No landmarks.

163
00:08:49,219 --> 00:08:53,104
A single light, with nothing to measure it against.

164
00:08:53,104 --> 00:08:57,066
Distance and speed are close to impossible to judge.

165
00:08:57,533 --> 00:08:59,405
Now what counts against it.

166
00:08:59,405 --> 00:09:05,230
Gorman followed the light for twenty-seven minutes, through dives, climbs and turns.

167
00:09:05,230 --> 00:09:07,379
A balloon drifts with the wind.

168
00:09:07,379 --> 00:09:10,500
It does not seem to turn away from a fighter.

169
00:09:10,966 --> 00:09:16,088
The controllers said the light increased its spacing from the Mustang.

170
00:09:16,088 --> 00:09:20,991
The pilot said it ran from him at up to four hundred miles an hour.

171
00:09:20,991 --> 00:09:23,333
A balloon cannot outrun an F-51.

172
00:09:23,799 --> 00:09:28,447
Ruppelt's illusion is clever, and it can explain the head-on passes.

173
00:09:28,447 --> 00:09:35,966
But it asks us to believe the passes were Gorman's own turns, and that he and six others misjudged the speeds.

174
00:09:36,433 --> 00:09:38,999
And McDonald's finding matters here.

175
00:09:38,999 --> 00:09:45,130
The very file that carries the balloon label also carries statements that discount it.

176
00:09:45,130 --> 00:09:46,698
The label is official.

177
00:09:46,698 --> 00:09:48,266
The reasoning is thin.

178
00:09:48,733 --> 00:09:52,399
So the balloon is real, and possibly part of the story.

179
00:09:52,399 --> 00:09:55,200
But it does not cleanly explain the speed.

180
00:09:55,200 --> 00:09:58,666
That leaves another suspect, and some stranger ones.

181
00:09:59,133 --> 00:10:01,848
Suspect two: Jupiter, and the others.

182
00:10:01,848 --> 00:10:06,031
Jupiter really was in the sky that night, bright and low.

183
00:10:06,031 --> 00:10:08,600
Sign and Grudge both pointed to it.

184
00:10:09,066 --> 00:10:14,836
Menzel argued a mirage could make Jupiter jump, and flicker, and seem to move.

185
00:10:14,836 --> 00:10:17,722
A mirage can change a light's position.

186
00:10:17,722 --> 00:10:20,533
It does not fly past a pilot's canopy.

187
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,190
Gorman flew at night for years.

188
00:10:23,190 --> 00:10:24,391
He knew a planet.

189
00:10:24,391 --> 00:10:29,408
Jupiter does not blink on and off, bank sharply, and shoot straight up.

190
00:10:29,408 --> 00:10:31,033
This one is a weak fit.

191
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:34,252
A third idea comes from the Cold War.

192
00:10:34,252 --> 00:10:35,815
A Soviet rocket test.

193
00:10:35,815 --> 00:10:40,279
Aerospace engineer Travis Taylor looks at it and rejects it.

194
00:10:40,279 --> 00:10:44,966
The range is wrong, and rockets of that era could not maneuver.

195
00:10:45,433 --> 00:10:52,767
That leaves the last suspect: a craft that nobody built, an intelligence flying with, quote, a definite thought.

196
00:10:52,767 --> 00:10:54,797
It would explain the maneuvers.

197
00:10:54,797 --> 00:11:00,233
It has no hardware, no photograph, and no second witness who could say what it was.

198
00:11:00,700 --> 00:11:02,172
Time to score the case.

199
00:11:02,172 --> 00:11:03,260
Not what we hope.

200
00:11:03,260 --> 00:11:04,733
What the file supports.

201
00:11:05,200 --> 00:11:05,726
Debrief.

202
00:11:05,726 --> 00:11:07,700
Time to score the Gorman case.

203
00:11:08,166 --> 00:11:11,407
A real flight and a real report: evidence.

204
00:11:11,407 --> 00:11:16,267
Six witnesses besides Gorman saw a fast, round light: evidence.

205
00:11:16,267 --> 00:11:20,280
A balloon was released ten minutes before: evidence.

206
00:11:20,280 --> 00:11:23,752
A balloon alone, fitting the speed: unlikely.

207
00:11:23,752 --> 00:11:26,066
A mirage of Jupiter: unlikely.

208
00:11:26,533 --> 00:11:30,314
A radioactive craft: debunked, it was cosmic rays.

209
00:11:30,314 --> 00:11:32,281
A Soviet rocket: debunked.

210
00:11:32,281 --> 00:11:35,533
A craft from elsewhere: no evidence at all.

211
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:36,823
The verdict.

212
00:11:36,823 --> 00:11:40,596
The sighting was real, and the witnesses were credible.

213
00:11:40,596 --> 00:11:45,330
A balloon is the best candidate, but it leaves the speed unexplained.

214
00:11:45,330 --> 00:11:47,799
Open case, narrowing but not closed.

215
00:11:48,266 --> 00:11:51,242
The strangest part is how ordinary it was.

216
00:11:51,242 --> 00:11:57,478
A pilot with time to spare, a clear Friday night, and a light that would not let him go.

217
00:11:57,478 --> 00:12:00,100
He never accepted the balloon answer.

218
00:12:00,566 --> 00:12:07,592
Next case: a farmhouse in Kentucky, nineteen fifty-five, a family, and little men at the window.

219
00:12:07,592 --> 00:12:09,788
A siege that lasted all night.

220
00:12:09,788 --> 00:12:10,666
Was it real?

221
00:12:11,133 --> 00:12:12,964
What do you think Gorman saw?

222
00:12:12,964 --> 00:12:18,266
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
